FINAL ANSWER:
Troy Goode owns the most surviving code. Douglas Christopher Wilson is second. I measured this with `git blame`, which credits each line to the commit that last changed it. Commit counts don't enter into it.

I ignored whitespace changes and tracked moved or copied lines. I also excluded the `62628c0` reformat commit. That commit re-indented every JS file. Without the exclusion, Fiona Formatter would own most of the JS. I skipped the lockfile and binary files. That leaves 1,995 lines across 18 files.

| Author | Lines | Share |
|---|---|---|
| Troy Goode | 810 | 41% |
| Douglas Christopher Wilson | 629 | 32% |
| Íñigo Marquínez Prado | 95 | 5% |
| Jon Church | 72 | 4% |
| Sebastian Beltran | 67 | 3% |
| Ulises Gascón | 52 | 3% |
| Ignacio Mangas | 44 | 2% |
| Everyone else | 226 | 11% |

- **Troy Goode** owns the core of the project. They hold `lib/index.js` (159 of 238 lines) and most of the test files, including `vary.js`, `error-response.js` and `example-app.js`. They also hold about 42% of `README.md`.
- **Douglas Christopher Wilson** owns the project scaffolding. That covers the lint config, `package.json`, `HISTORY.md`, the license and about half of `ci.yml`. They also hold 339 lines of `test/test.js`, which is close to Troy's 317.
- **Everyone else** mostly owns a single area. Sebastian Beltran has `codeql.yml`, Íñigo Marquínez Prado has `scorecard.yml` and Ulises Gascón has `dependabot.yml`. Jon Church has about a quarter of the README.

**Who is still active:** Troy's last commit was 2026-04-10, and Douglas's was 2021-09-07. I haven't checked whether Douglas is still around. If they've left, about a third of the code, mostly config and CI, has no active owner.

**Limits:** Surviving lines show who last touched code, not who understands it. Troy's most recent commits are `lib: annotate handlers` and `test: vary header coverage`. Some of that line count may come from rewrites of earlier code.

I didn't use the `busfactor` skill. It weights commits by age, so it measures recent activity rather than surviving code. I can run it if you also want a CODEOWNERS file and an orphaned-zone report.
